\layout Subsection*

Answer
\layout Subsubsection*

Case 1) 
\begin_inset Formula $k\leq m$
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

When the system's state, 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

, is less than or equal to the number of servers we have boundary flow as
 in Figure\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{cap:flowAtBoundaryMMma}

\end_inset 

,
\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename ./diagrams/boundaryFlowMMinf.eps
	width 40text%

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

Flow rates at a boundary.
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{cap:flowAtBoundaryMMma}

\end_inset 


\end_inset 

which leads to the following balance equation 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\lambda P\left(k-1\right)=k\mu P\left(k\right).\]

\end_inset 

As a result, for 
\begin_inset Formula $k=0\dots m$
\end_inset 

 we may write
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
P\left(k\right)=\frac{\lambda}{k\mu}P\left(k-1\right).\label{eq:PkForPartAExercise}\end{equation}

\end_inset 


\layout Subsubsection*

Case 2) 
\begin_inset Formula $m<k\leq n$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Standard

The detailed balance for this case is
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\frac{\lambda}{m\mu}P\left(k-1\right)\]

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

When the system's state is greater than 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula $k>m$
\end_inset 

 the flow at the boundary is as in Figure\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{cap:flowAtBoundaryMMmb}

\end_inset 

, the detailed balance equation is then
\begin_inset Float figure
