Flow rates at a boundary.
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{cap:flowAtBoundaryMMmb}

\end_inset 


\end_inset 

which leads to 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\lambda P(k-1)=m\mu P\left(k\right)\]

\end_inset 


\layout Enumerate

For each of the two cases outlined in the previous part write down the steady
 state distribution as a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(0\right)$
\end_inset 

, the probability of the system being empty.
 
\begin_deeper 
\layout Subsection*

Answer 
\layout Subsubsection*

Case 1) 
\begin_inset Formula $k\leq m$
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

From Detailed Balance we may write
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\frac{\lambda}{k\mu}P\left(k-1\right).\]

\end_inset 

And defining 
\begin_inset Formula \[
a=\frac{\lambda}{\mu}\]

\end_inset 

which in turn gives 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\frac{\lambda}{k\mu}=\frac{a}{k}\]

\end_inset 

which we substitute back into the detailed balance obtaining
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\frac{a}{k}P\left(k-1\right)\]

\end_inset 

and as before
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray}
P\left(k\right)=\frac{a^{k}}{k!}P\left(0\right) & \textrm{for} & k\leq m\label{eq:probabilitiesExercise}\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset 


\layout Subsubsection*

Case 2) 
\begin_inset Formula $m<k\leq n$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Standard

The detailed balance for this case is
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\frac{\lambda}{m\mu}P\left(k-1\right)\]

\end_inset 

substituting for 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset 

 gives
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\frac{a}{m}P\left(k-1\right)\]

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

leading to 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\left(\frac{a}{m}\right)^{k-m}P\left(m\right).\]

\end_inset 

Finally we use (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eq:probabilitiesExercise}

\end_inset 

) and substitute 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset 

 to find 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(m\right)$
\end_inset 

 which leads to
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(m\right)=\frac{a^{m}}{m!}P\left(0\right)\]

\end_inset 

therefore 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
P\left(k\right)=\frac{a^{k}}{m^{k-m}m!}P\left(0\right) & \textrm{for} & k>m\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

thus we can write
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\left\{ \begin{array}{cc}
P\left(0\right)\frac{a^{k}}{k!}, & k\leq m\\
P\left(0\right)\frac{a^{k}}{m^{k-m}m!} & k>m\end{array}\right.\]

\end_inset 


\end_deeper 
\layout Enumerate

Use the fact that the steady state distribution must be normalised to show
 that 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(0\right)=\left[\sum_{k=0}^{m}\frac{a^{k}}{k!}+\sum_{k=m+1}^{n}\frac{a^{k}}{m!m^{k-m}}\right]^{-1}\]

\end_inset 

where 
\begin_inset Formula $a=\frac{\lambda}{\mu}$
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

 is the arrival rate and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset 

 is the rate of the service time distribution.
